  The fork: [1mhow long the stored identity outlives the moment of login.[0m This is a security-posture decision, not a
  storage-API preference: it sets how long a walk-away or a shared machine stays logged in, and it's what an XSS bug
  would be able to read.

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives reload, dies when the tab closes. Buys continuity across navigation without leaving an
    identity behind on a shared machine. Costs: each tab is its own island, and closing the tab logs you out. Readable
    by any script on the page.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives tab close, browser restart, indefinitely until explicitly cleared. Buys "still logged in
    tomorrow." Costs: an identity that persists on disk with no expiry; on a shared machine the next person inherits it.
    Also readable by any script on the page.
  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — a module-level variable, gone on reload. Buys the smallest exposure: nothing on disk, nothing for
    XSS to scrape from storage. Costs: a page refresh loses the identity, which for a login flow is usually
    unacceptable.

  One thing that applies to all three: none of them is a substitute for a server-side session. A client-stored user id
  says "this browser claims to be user X" — it is fine for [3mtracking who logged in[0m, which is what you asked for, and must
  not later become the basis for deciding what someone is allowed to do. I'll write that constraint into the spec
  explicitly.

  I recommend [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m. It's the only option that survives reload without creating an identity that silently
  outlives the person's use of the machine, and switching to [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m later is a one-line change if you decide you
  want longer-lived login.
